<h2>The procedural route, step by step</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>FIR under the SC/ST Act</strong> — once provisions of the 1989 Act are invoked, bail jurisdiction vests in the Special Court or Exclusive Special Court constituted under Section 14.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Application before the Special Court</strong> — the appellant sought anticipatory bail (A.B.P. No. 230 of 2025) before the Additional Sessions Judge-1 (SC/ST Act), Jamui, which refused it on 27 March 2025.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Appeal under Section 14-A(2)</strong> — the refusal was carried to the Patna High Court as a criminal appeal, the statutory substitute for a fresh application.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Appellate order</strong> — the High Court set aside the refusal and directed release on anticipatory bail upon arrest or surrender within six weeks, on bonds of ₹5,000 with two sureties, subject to Section 482(2) BNSS conditions.</div>
</div>

<h2>Land disputes wearing criminal clothing</h2>
<p>The factual matrix is one Indian criminal practice knows intimately: a long-running land dispute, an altercation during ploughing, cross-allegations between two sides, and an FIR that names a cluster of family members with specific violence attributed to one. The appellant\'s defence emphasised exactly these features — general and omnibus allegations, the overt act assigned to someone else, the mutual character of the clash, and the pendency of the investigation. The court recorded these submissions, including the service-of-notice position, and granted relief. The six-week surrender window is a Patna High Court convention worth noting: protection is conditioned on the accused submitting to the court\'s jurisdiction within a defined period, preventing indefinite evasion under an open-ended order.</p>

<h2>Where the Section 18 bar begins and ends</h2>
<p>Section 18 of the SC/ST Act declares that Section 438 CrPC — now Section 482 BNSS — does not apply to offences under the Act. But the Supreme Court\'s jurisprudence has settled that the bar presupposes a prima facie case: courts retain the duty to examine whether the FIR\'s allegations genuinely attract the Act\'s ingredients, including whether the alleged insult or abuse occurred within public view and whether the caste element is specific or formulaic. Where that examination shows an essentially civil or property quarrel with the Act\'s provisions appended, anticipatory bail jurisdiction survives. Orders like the present one apply that framework through the appellate lens of Section 14-A(2).</p>

<h2>Practice notes</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>Compute limitation carefully — a Section 14-A appeal has its own timeline, and delay needs explanation. Plead the civil backdrop with documents: khatiyan entries, mutation records and pending title suits convert an assertion of false implication into a demonstrable one. Address the overt-act mapping squarely, because the contrast between a named assailant and omnibus co-accused is often the single most persuasive feature. And once protection is granted, honour the surrender window and bond conditions strictly; breach revives custody at a stroke.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>The equivalent of Section 438 CrPC is Section 482 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, and bail conditions are governed by Section 482(2). For occurrences before 1 July 2024, the IPC offence numbering continues to apply to the substantive charges, as in this case.</p>
</div>
